        "answer_markdown": "**(a) Nominal GDP in 2026** uses 2026 quantities and prices:\n\n\\[(40\\times 5)+(12\\times 15)=200+180=\\boxed{380\\text{ currency units}}.\\]\n\n**(b) Real GDP in 2026** uses 2025 base-year prices with 2026 quantities:\n\n\\[(40\\times 4)+(12\\times 12)=160+144=\\boxed{304\\text{ currency units}}.\\]\n\n**(c) GDP deflator and inflation**\n\n2025 nominal GDP (also real GDP in the base year) is \\((30\\times4)+(10\\times12)=240\\).\n\n\\[\\text{2026 GDP deflator}=\\frac{380}{304}\\times100=\\boxed{125}.\\]\n\nThe 2025 deflator is 100, so inflation from 2025 to 2026 is \\(\\frac{125-100}{100}\\times100=\\boxed{25\\%}\\).\n\n**(d)** With the nominal wage unchanged while the price level rises by 25%, the wage’s purchasing power **falls**. The same amount of money buys fewer goods and services than in 2025.\n\n**(e)** The technology improvement shifts the production possibilities curve outward, indicating greater productive capacity for both wheat and books.",
